[07:02] I built and saved the phone agent for `briefs/pl-g7-multi-raw.md`, but it has not been tested in a live call. `public-test-set.json` has no practice customers for this brief, so I did not run `tools/run_cases.py`. My checks were the lowering step, the workflow checker (0 errors, 0 warnings), a local run of the date-calculation code on sample products, and a reading of the workflow against every rule in the brief.
[07:02] 
[07:02] **How I read the brief:**
[07:02] - **Memo OM-17 applies.** 21 October 2026 is a bank holiday and 26 October is a normal business day. A car loan's deadline is never later than the 5th business day after today.
[07:02] - **Memo OM-3 does not apply.** It is dated before the policy extract, so the extract replaces it.
[07:02] - **The multi-product annex applies to every customer, including those with one product.** Its rule 1 says `get_account` returns one to three products. So the agent calls `add_note` for every product without a promise, single-product customers included. This is the main guess in the build. If the bank means "Brief A never uses `add_note`" for single-product customers, that one rule changes.
[07:02] 
[07:02] **How the agent works:**
[07:02] - **Dates and amounts are worked out in code, not by the model.** For each product: band, deadline, floor, the two offers it may make, and every valid promise date. It also gets the callback window and a calendar that resolves "this Friday" and "next Friday".
[07:02] - **One tool per product.** `create_promise_N` and `add_note_N` already contain that product's `product_id`, so the model cannot invent one. Each transfer queue (restructuring, support, general) has its own fixed tool.
[07:02] - **Every ending in the payment step comes from tool results, not from the model's choice.**
[07:02]   - Any recorded promise closes as `promise`.
[07:02]   - With no promise, the close reason is that of the first product whose note is not `already_paid`.
[07:02]   - If every product is noted `already_paid`, the call closes as `paid`.
[07:02]   - Any transfer closes as `transferred` with that queue's reason, even after a recorded promise.
[07:02] - **Fixed closing lines.** Each ending speaks a fixed farewell. For example, the promise ending says "You will receive a text message confirming this promise."
[07:02] - **Notes come last.** The prompt tells the agent to add notes only after all promises are recorded, and never before a transfer.
[07:02] 
[07:02] **Known gaps:**
[07:02] - A request for a human before the customer confirms their name is not handled. Rule 42 is handled from the identity check onward.
[07:02] - What happens when a transfer or callback booking fails twice is not in the brief. I close the call with the transfer or `no_callback` outcome anyway.
[07:02] 
